ing them keeping  them stable  all that good stuff and this is an  organization  that is run by adorable baby animals  and that's great that's that's exactly  what we need right now  anyway everything is fine if this  project proceeds  linearly going from data engineer hands  off the data to the data scientist who  hands off  their model to the software engineer but  like of course  this never works um i mean as you can  see the authors of this figure have  have drawn in these little little arrows  suggesting some recursion  and indeed that is that is pretty  standard  so there's a couple ways that this kind  of linear flow breaks down  one is that really really great machine  learning models often take you know  quite a few  iterations and feedback and that  feedback can come in the form of  you know maybe our data set is changing  or something about the application is  changing  or it could be that the system  constraints are you know quite  particular and data scientists have to  work with that so they get feedback from  software engineers  um and then they might have several  iterations  of discussing what the model will look  like in production and feeding that  input back into the data scientist work  also data scientists need to be able to  anticipate the constraints  that software engineers will have so  they really need to be able to you know  to see or understand in some way if the  you know the dependencies of the project  that they're developing  locally will work in production  and everybody on the team needs to know  where the data came from and i'm so  sorry that it looks like my head is  cutting off  the baby animal labels but hopefully  from context you can get it  so there's a lot of back and forth a lot  of circularity  and this can all get quite confusing and  complex and it becomes  a thing to be managed in and of itself  but machine learning is although it's  new to the software engineering stack  we have you know a lot of practices from  software engineering  that are great at dealing with  complexity um and  specifically we can look to the field of  devops for some  inspiration so devops is  putting together developers and  operations  so people developers are people that  want to develop cool features they want  to experiment they want to make things  fast  kind of like a data scientist can try  out a bunch of different models  and then ops people that need to keep  the system stable and running with no  interruption so we can think of that as  more of like our engineering guys and  girls  so devops had a pretty big revolution  you know in the last decade or so and  it's now a mainstay of  you know most major tech companies that  they have a lot of devops practices  um but machine learning uh you know it's  still because it's new we're still  figuring out what is a team that works  on a full staff machine learning project  going to be like um  there's not as many devops principles  and standards that are you know  ingrained in the same way as in software  development  um but we have some we're interested in  seeing how well those ideas can extend  and in particular we're really  interested in this big idea of  continuous  integration and delivery which i will  explain and we're going to abbreviate  this ci cd  so cicd is a practice that establishes  collaboration rules between developers  and operations specialists and it's part  philosophy  and part practice so let's take a look  at that means let's get you know a  little less abstract  the idea is that you have a code base  for your project  and whenever there's a change in your  source code  a build will happen automatically and  then some tests get run on that build  and if the build passes the test then  the modified source code becomes a  candidate to be merged into the main  branch of your project  and if you fail then you can keep  iterating on this  and you might be able to tell from the  way i'm talking that this is often  done with git that 