we take out a branch  a new branch to try out some new  features and when we push that branch  when we make this pull request  to our central you know our ci server um  then we will be getting the test run and  then  if everything goes well then we can  merge the branch  into the main part of the project and so  we're using a git flow where the main  branch is  presumably what will go to production  and we use branches to experiment  so the idea here is that actions like  git commits and pushes will trigger  feedback  and every round of feedback can produce  a release  candidate because we're always testing  and because we're always preparing to  release something  we're like it's really fast to turn  around something new the idea is to get  out of like  you know having to wait months and  months to test something out because  you know to get it ready to go into  production because  every day you're used to testing so  let's take a look at what's different  when machine learning is part of your  workflow  so a couple things first of all it's not  enough to monitor changes oh sorry  here we go it's not enough to monitor  changes in your code base anymore you  also have to monitor changes in your  data set so  machine learning models are their sort  of code  and their sort of data they are a  function  but they're you know it's they're not  really that defined by their code on a  lot of applications and  particularly in deep learning it seems  like  data is what really drives the result of  what  you know what the model is like so we  have to monitor for changes in our data  set because if the training data changes  that's as good as changing your model  another difference is that instead of  building software  we're not you know we're no longer  compiling we're training  models or anything for training them you  know it's  it's intensive sometimes um you know a  lot of the computers that can run  a standard build for software  development  are just not up to training a deep  learning model or even  a quite large you know linear model um  if you've got to do a really  big matrix decomposition you don't  always want to do that on  the computers you have lying around  sometimes you're going to need some  hardware  or just some special cuda drivers you  know it's not trivial  another difference is that when we run  tests you know  we have these these we're actually  evaluating  the model you know we're going to maybe  the way we evaluate it is not like these  pass  fail kind of things it's like  i just gave it some new data some held  out data and i want to know how my new  model performed and we have to be kind  of detailed  and you know investigative about it  because of  just knowing a single score like okay  did my accuracy  improve on the new model is not really  enough because we may have in fact  lost something like we've we've reduced  precision  you know and perhaps that's really not  okay for our  application and so this kind of  evaluation of did my model get better  actually requires some domain knowledge  both about  what you know how data science works and  about the problem that you're working on  so these are things that you know  does not do not really make sense to  binarize into checks when you  we really want a more conclusive and  detailed report about how the model  performs  so why don't we have this yet a few  reasons  first of all version control can't  always track changes in data sets  so like i had talked about a lot of this  is very get driven it's very git  centric and you know a lot of data sets  are not things that you want to be  keeping  in your github repository um data sets  are frequently  on the you know a bit too large for  traditional version control so we need a  way to deal with that  if they're going to be triggers as well  as code changes  next is that training models isn't like  building software so again  um sometimes our our cpus that we have  around  are just not enough and we need a 