itical  for machine learning projects  um so because we're in the git ecosystem  our attention has been caught by  a couple of developments in that world  we have github actions which is a  continuous integration  system that is supported by github so  it's basically uh anytime your github  repository detects a change  they will provision a computer for you  and you can run whatever workflow you  want it is really cool  and there's a neat marketplace of  actions that you can check out  and there's also get lab ci cd um again  my head is cutting this off but get lab  ci  is a wonderful suite it's it's really  i think it's like one of their  specialties it's a beautiful ci system  so both of these  for the purposes that i'm going to talk  to about today  are interchangeable we've developed the  tool that i'm going to show you to work  with both of them  and i'm going to kind of give the  examples and the rest of this talk in  github because of  just because of it's completely free so  it's maybe a little bit easier for  somebody who's totally new to this to  get in but everything i'm going to show  you works fine on both of these and  there is  no difference in what you can do in  these systems as far as my talk is  concerned  okay so here's our project name  continuous machine learning  we have started an open source project  about using continuous integration from  these two systems github actions and get  lab ci and we're planning on expanding  to some other  common providers um with machine  learning  so continuous machine learning here's  our beautiful logo and there's two ways  that you can follow our project one of  them is on the  cml.dev website and we spent a lot of  time developing like  use cases here um okay  oh there we go we spent some time  developing use cases  um you know so all different kind of  projects that are ready to go out of the  box that you can copy and paste to your  own project  and try making them for both github and  gitlab  um and we've got a repo on github that  you can  come in open issues make a pull request  for a new feature  um it's it's pretty active and it's got  tutorials all that  okay so let's revisit this figure  so github actions where does this fit in  um i'm going to highlight it it's this  part  so what github actions does is that it  can automate  training and testing a model and then  return the results to you in your pull  request  by default this workflow so whatever is  your training job can be run on runner  machines provided by github  but you can also use your own machines  too so cml  works with github actions so cml is not  a new  continuous integration system it's kind  of a library and a set of methods that  you can use with either github actions  or gitlab ci  to help adapt those services to work  with machine learning because right now  github actions is really designed mostly  with software development  in mind but we think it's quite flexible  quite powerful  and can offer a lot here too so i'm  going to take us through  how the priorities that we laid out  earlier show up  in cml so first let's talk about  providing feedback in the form of metric  reports  okay so let's say that we have a project  in github and we're going to use github  actions to automate  training and evaluating a model um so  what we do is we create this  special yaml file that's always going to  be in the same file path  you just place it in this github  workflows file  and this is the secret sauce for any  github action no matter how simple or  complicated  you create a yaml file here and you're  going to give it some  you know a couple of arguments in the  yaml format  and give it some instructions to run and  then whatever you say is what  github will do when you when you it  detects a change  so we have our file here we're selecting  some triggers  so for me i'm saying my trigger is going  to be push when the repository detects a  push  then this workflow is going to be run i  am specifying a docker container here so  i'm pulling first uh the operating  system for the runner  and that's for the machine that github  is provisioning for me  a runner managed by them i'm pulling the  docker container for the cml project  you can also use your own docker  container or you can use no docker  container  and so when this happens we're going to  run the following workflow we will  install some requirements  we're going to run a python train dot pi  is my script  and then we're going to uh use some cml  functions with and a little bit of batch  scripting here  um it's it's fairly mundane stuff and  then we're going to use this to write  a markdown file report.markdown  and we're going to use cml to deliver  that markdown  file and i'll show you what that means  essentially  when you make a pull request you get a  report  and it appears as a comment in the pull  request so  here you can see that we've got you know  we made a commit  on this pull request and we printed out  the accuracy of the model that was  trained by pythontrain.pi  and we printed out a picture and the  picture is an ordinary  png file it's not special in any way  and so the value of having this appear  in your pull request  is that now this result from your model  training  is visible to your team and when you  decide if you want to  merge this model into your you know the  main branch of your project  you have this available to you and it's  always linked to the commit  that generated it so  hopefully when you have this you and  your team can have a more informed  discussion  and a conversation about whether or not  this is merge worthy  let's take a closer look at what exactly  is run in the workflow  we have a training script that outputs a  text file of  metrics and a confusion matrix so the  first thing we do is we do some bash  scripting  to take the contents of metrics.text put  them in the markdown report  then we're going to use this publish  function that we we've created here  to take your confusion matrix png host  it somewhere  and put it in the report and then we  deliver the report with cml to your pull  request  okay here's another example where i'm  using dvc  to make a table that compares model  performance metrics from the last  experiment with the master branch of the  project  so dvc you don't have to use cml with  dvc for the record they are totally  distinct  but because of dvc creates tools to do  things like  a diff for model performance across  two different git branches i think it  fits really naturally with cml  and anything else that you're doing with  git flow so if you're doing git flow and  data science  i think dvc is very natural but i'm very  biased here  this is my team so here i'm using dvc  metrics diff to create a table that says  how the metrics on my current branch  compares to what's on the main branch of  the project  and i'm also using dvc plots to plot the  difference in the loss functions  and fyi metrics are dvc plots are just a  wrapper for vega light plots  um so this would appear in your pull  request and here's another example using  tensorboard so we wrote a helper  function  so that when you open the pull request  you'll get a link to your  tensorboard.dev page  where you can track your model training  in real time so if your model is  training on these remote computers  you can still see it training on a  tensorboard  and there's a ton of flexibility here  you can pretty much play anything that  can go  in a github markdown document so like  anything that you could imagine putting  on like the readme of a project  um can go in one of these cml reports  um so you could be making your plots  using whatever visualization library you  prefer it could be seaborn or ggplot  vago whatever and likewise any way of  generating a table too  so this works with every programming  language because of it's very high level  and it's quite agnostic  um so python and r even though i'm only  showing you python examples  it works quite well with r  okay so we've been exploring a couple of  ways to use these reports like in what  situations do they provide the most  value and there's a couple cases that  we've considered  so one as in the example i showed you is  that you can use the report  to tell you about how model training  went on your remote machine so it could  be either to use it with tensorboard to  track model training in real time on a  remote machine that was  you know if your training job was  triggered by the continuous integration  system  um or it could be to simply return um  you know some of this model evaluation  like what does the confusion matrix with  my trained model look like  it could be on your training data or on  your validation data  um i really like transparent  reproducible and detailed test results i  think that you know  even though we're just at the beginning  of what we can put in a report and  thinking about it i think that  the real strength is coming up with  really descriptive and interesting tests  to put here  and one thing to note is that you don't  have to train your model  using the ci system you can just train  it locally  and then check it into ci when you're  ready so only commit the model that you  like the most  and then this could be a test suite so  you can do things that are akin to like  a unit test on your model or your data  set  and only report the results of of that  so you don't  have to be training in the ci system  to get the value of testing  and finally we can also look at  reviewing data and models like code so  if we have ideas about like model  metrics diffs or a data diff  if we are reviewing you know on a pull  request maybe the data set has changed  and you know we want to make a diff to  see how far has it drifted  from where it is on the main branch um  all of these things can be done and  we're still  building and exploring use cases around  it so the idea here is that similar to  how we  you know might open