 you can just  pass some credentials to your workflow  and then you can do things like dvc  pulled data dvc  repro so reproduce the pipeline if the  data set has changed and then you can  also use dvc to  push results from your ci run so if you  created a new model  on your ci run you can use dvc push to  push it into cloud storage  so pull data what it's doing is it's  going into the cloud pulling the csv  putting it on the runner and then dvc  push is going from the runner  to the cloud okay so some consequences  for continuous integration here  first changes in your data set can now  trigger a github action  even if the data set itself isn't part  of git  github actions can now push and pull  very large artifacts to and from cloud  storage  and data and model versions are linked  to every model training run  and because of you can start using  things like s3 storage  you know any you don't have you can have  these serverless  storage methods that means that you have  one less server  potentially in your workflow and that's  great  all right our third priority is that we  wanted to be able to let our ci system  allocate cloud resources to train  models and really quickly because this  one is is super cool but it's actually  we're changing the approach right now  and so i don't want to over provide  details on something that's going to  change  but um we've set up something right now  with docker machine  that basically means that anytime you  push you will deploy  cloud resources so you can actually  deploy an ec2 instance and it will turn  on and turn itself off after it's done  running your job  and we're still we're reworking this a  bit right now with terraform it is super  cool  um but it's not ready today but  basically with this  this approach you know in the way that  we have it right now we've done things  like  style transfer model on an ec2 instance  that has a gpu and a cool thing is  you don't have to worry about  remembering to turn off your gpu  or 